          dealing: "Cards are shuffled, cut by the player to dealer's right, and dealt one at a time until each player has five cards. The remainder forms a draw pile, with the top card turned face up to start the discard pile.",
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Collect the best five-card poker hand and be the first to knock when confident of having the strongest hand.',
          gameplay: "1. **Ante**: Each player pays one chip to the central pot before the deal.\n2. **Turn Structure**: Players take turns clockwise, starting with the player to dealer's left.\n3. **Drawing**: On your turn, draw one card either from the top of the stock pile (face down) or take the top card of the face-up discard pile.\n4. **Knocking (Optional)**: If you believe you have the best poker hand, you may knock by knocking on the table or saying 'I knock'. This signals that play will end after each other player has one more turn.\n5. **Discarding**: After drawing (and optionally knocking), discard one card face up on the discard pile. You cannot discard the same card you just took from the discard pile in the same turn.\n6. **Immediate Knock**: A player satisfied with their original dealt hand may knock on their first turn without drawing or discarding.",
          keyRules: [
            'Draw from stock or discard pile each turn',
            'Must discard after drawing',
            'Can knock to end round',
            'Best poker hand wins the pot',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Know your poker hand rankings thoroughly. Knock when you have at least a strong pair or better, especially if few cards have been drawn from the stock.',
          intermediate: 'Watch the discard pile carefully. If you see high cards being discarded, it suggests opponents are building specific hands. If you see low cards discarded, opponents may be aiming for straights or flushes.',
          advanced: "Timing your knock is critical. Knock early with a decent hand if you think opponents haven't had time to improve. Wait longer if you have a very strong hand and want to lure opponents into staying in. Consider the pot size - a larger pot justifies knocking with a slightly weaker hand.",
          tips: [
            "Don't be afraid to fold after a knock if your hand is weak - losing 1 chip is better than losing 2 plus the chance at the pot.",
            "If you draw from the discard pile, you're revealing information about what you're collecting. Use the stock pile for secrecy.",
            "The player who knocked has an advantage in knowing exactly when play ends - use this if you're the knocker.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
